  "scientific_findings": {
    "root_cause_of_extreme_diff": "Extreme differences (> +30 dB and < -30 dB) are driven almost exclusively by pixels where backscatter falls below the Sentinel-1 Noise Equivalent Sigma Zero (NESZ, approx -22 to -28 dB). Following thermal noise removal, smooth calm water produces residual linear values of 10^-5 to 10^-11 (-50 to -106 dB). When differencing logarithmic values (Delta_dB = July_dB - June_dB), dividing by a 10^-11 denominator yields differences of +70 to +91 dB.",
    "spatial_concentration": "Pixels below -35 dB and extreme difference anomalies are spatially concentrated along the primary river channel of the Jamuna, permanent water oxbows, and swath boundary interpolation edges. They are not random sensor noise.",
    "recommendation_for_phase2b": "For Phase 2B, do not clip physical observations. Instead, in thresholding and flood mapping: (1) Apply adaptive speckle filtering (Refined Lee 5x5 or Gamma MAP) in linear power before dB conversion to stabilize the noise floor; (2) In threshold classification, all values below the water mode threshold (e.g. < -23 dB VH, < -15 dB VV) are classified as water regardless of whether they are -25 dB or -100 dB, rendering the deep negative tail completely harmless to binary flood classification."
  }
